WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.640
[Music]

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:24.300
Not so many years ago, travel between continents was a major undertaking and few persons had either the time or the money required for such long journeys.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:32.180
Today, air transportation has reduced the world in size until no area on Earth is more than 60 hours away.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.680
This change has brought many new problems.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:41.620
Preventing the introduction of foreign diseases into the United States is one of the most important of these problems.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:50.940
Within seven hours flying time of the United States, there are areas in which dengue and yellow fever are endemic.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.960
From the jungles of Africa and South America, these diseases may be brought to our shores by air travelers.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:15.000
Most of our ports of entry are within areas where the Aedes aegypti mosquito, vector of both dengue and yellow fever, is already present.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:27.820
Our first line of defense against these diseases is the quarantine officer. It is his duty to prevent the entry into this country of infected passengers or mosquitoes.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:38.240
Essential preventive measures are, one, the destruction of insects brought in with the plane, and identification of those that are found.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:48.500
Two, checking vaccination certificates of persons originating in or passing through yellow fever countries.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.760
And three, careful examination of passengers.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:06.480
Our second line of defense is the control of the Aedes aegypti mosquito breeding. This is the job of the aegypti control inspector.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:21.580
In many South Atlantic and Gulf Coast cities, the aegypti control unit is working to reduce the hazard of introduction of dengue and yellow fever.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:33.320
In order to successfully apply proper control measures, the inspector must be familiar with the habits of this sly, biting mosquito.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.620
The Aedes aegypti mosquito breeds in and around homes.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:44.660
It rarely flies more than a hundred yards.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.740
Aedes aegypti eggs are generally found at the water level on the sides of containers.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.020
These are the larvae, commonly known as "wiggletails."

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:09.860
These are the pupae, with an adult emerging. There are four stages in the life cycle of the mosquito: egg, larva, pupa, and adult.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.400
About ten days are required to complete the cycle from egg to adult.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:36.780
Notice the white-tipped palps, the triangular white spots on the abdomen, the white legbands, and the white flyer-shaped design on the thorax.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.800
These are characteristic of the adult aegypti mosquito.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.540
This is a group of inspectors similar to the group of which you are a member.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:04:00.600
After you understand the habits of the mosquito and the methods of control, you will become an important member of the aegypti control program.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:09.760
The necessary equipment is simple: notebook, report forms, larvicide applicator, flashlight,

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.540
pipette, chalk, dipper, mirror, kerosene, pointed hammer, and satchel.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:23.140
To completely eliminate aegypti breeding, an inspection zone must be covered every ten days.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:29.000
This period represents the life cycle of Aedes aegypti from egg to adult.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.860
As you work through your zone, use special markings so that you can be located easily by your supervisor.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.820
Before entering a premise to make an inspection, mark the first half of a "V" on the sidewalk or fence.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.560
Regardless of the type home you enter, always be pleasant and courteous.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.680
Once inside, your work begins.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:15.720
You will recognize these as aegypti larvae. The S-shaped swimming motion is characteristic of this species.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.720
Encourage the housewife to grow plants in earth rather than in water.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.740
Take larval specimens so that identification may be confirmed.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:44.840
Remove the plant and scrub the edges of the bowl to destroy the eggs. Rinse the roots thoroughly to eliminate larvae.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:59.800
Ant guards may be a source of aegypti breeding. In this case, they are treated with kerosene.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.480
Barrels and drums are frequent sources of aegypti breeding.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:13.720
For best visibility, use a mirror to reflect sunlight into dark containers.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:20.220
Avoid jarring the container before dipping, because aegypti larvae will quickly dive to the bottom if disturbed.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:35.640
Larvae are concentrated at the bottom of the dipper by pouring off excess water.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.660
This facilitates the transfer of larvae from dipper to vial.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:56.700
Kerosene is applied in only two cases. First, where poisonous larvicides might be dangerous,

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:01.100
and second, to water in which the non-feeding pupae are found.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:08.140
In areas where water is scarce, cisterns are used to store rainwater.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:23.260
Cisterns may be prolific breeding places because they contain water throughout the year.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:39.520
Examine trash buckets. Rainwater or wastewater is often found in such containers and, if not too dirty, aegypti will breed there.

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.860
Larvicide all receptacles of this type, or turn them "bottoms up."

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:05.460
Old discarded tires containing water are frequent breeding places. After removing the water, larvicide and store under cover.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:17.000
Tell the householder about the conditions found and how to prevent mosquito breeding.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.260
A completed "V" will indicate that the inspection is finished.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:33.160
Make a full report after each inspection.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:47.900
When beginning a new block, indicate the direction of travel, the date and time of day, and your initials, so that you can be easily located.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:55.120
No home is immune to Aedes aegypti as long as water containers are present.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:06.440
Housewives will soon learn to recognize you. The invitation to enter is usually cordial.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:13.760
If not, either explain the purpose of your visit more fully or thank the householder and refer the matter to the foreman.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.420
Water standing in flower containers is a definite hazard.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:28.380
Even when no breeding is found, request the housewife to change the water at least once every five days as a preventive measure.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:36.480
When breeding is found, the stem should be rinsed to remove larvae and the container scrubbed to eliminate attached eggs.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:41.720
Phenothiazine may be added to fresh water to prevent breeding.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:46.020
After the interior has been examined, inspect the yard.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:58.000
In the search for water containers look under, over, around, and, if possible, through any object you encounter.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:24.800
When no one is at home, proceed to examine the yard. Call back later to inspect the house.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:37.380
Breeding containers such as paint buckets, unused washtubs, and tin cans are frequently found around the yard.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:43.100
Work in a routine and careful manner to avoid overlooking any breeding places.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:48.440
Even though no larvae or pupae are found in a container, the water should be emptied and the container inverted.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:56.600
When not in use, septic tanks often hold accumulations of clear water in which aegypti will breed.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.500
Complete the "V", but box it to indicate that the interior has not been examined.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.500
Every premise in the zone must be examined.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:22.600
A thorough search is made in all vacant buildings.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:33.620
Sometimes containers are placed under leaky roofs to catch rainwater.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:40.260
Such containers must be inspected, emptied, and treated with larvicide to prevent breeding.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:48.900
Openings to a cistern are often difficult to locate. They may be concealed under floor coverings, furniture, or trash.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:55.900
In such places may be hidden mother foci that produce aegypti continuously and infest an entire neighborhood.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.600
Fill out a request for special corrective measures.

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:13.900
Unused closet bowls and flush tanks should be treated with a larvicide since they are frequent breeding places.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:28.000
Dispose of tin cans or puncture them so they won't hold water.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.100
Complete the circuit of the grounds.

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:46.200
A day's inspection will probably include the reporting of accumulations of tin cans such as this.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:51.100
After the day's activities, report to headquarters.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.920
Identification of specimens is checked by the supervisor.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:11.700
The presence of pupae indicate imminent emergence of adults. In such cases, adult spraying is necessary on the following day.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:19.440
At the beginning of each day's work, foremen of special crews are assigned work which inspectors are unable to handle,

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:25.580
such as large scale larviciding, introduction of fish, or permanent corrective measures.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:36.860
A clean-up crew may be necessary when refuse is not collected by other municipal agencies.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:45.300
Records show that three-fourths of all aegypti breeding occurs in tin cans, bottles, and other refuse.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:50.460
Not only is breeding prevalent in such containers, but eggs remain there through the winter.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:00.940
Special equipment is necessary for larviciding scrap piles.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:10.660
An abandoned cistern, such as this, may need a light film of oil or stocking with fish.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:17.340
The fish crew is probably the most important single service unit.

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:24.700
Several species of the genus gambusia, commonly called top minnows or mosquito fish, will eat the larvae.

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:32.500
Usually these minnows are collected from local ponds. Cisterns have been known to remain stocked with gambusia for several years.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:39.100
A watertight marine lamp is useful when treating dark, secluded places.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:55.520
When the only opening into a cistern is the lead-in pipe from the roof gutter or the air vent,

00:14:55.880 --> 00:15:01.200
fish can be poured into a rubber hose and flushed into the cistern with additional water.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:04.100
Cisterns in use should have all openings screened.

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:07.660
This is a well-sealed cistern.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.800
Unused cisterns should be destroyed or filled.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:25.900
A special crew inspects all roof gutters. One of the main jobs is to keep the gutters clear of leaves and branches,

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:30.380
thus preventing accumulations of water in which aegypti may breed.

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:36.900
When water is found, it is swept out.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:45.200
A maritime crew may be needed in seaports to inspect boats.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:49.000
A light film of kerosene will control breeding in water kegs.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:54.500
Cabins are sprayed to kill adults.

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:00.900
Flower containers in cemeteries are ideal mosquito-breeding places.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.840
A special assignment may be necessary for effective control.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:18.600
The continued presence of adult mosquitoes after all routine control measures have been employed

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:22.700
indicates some undetected breeding place or mother focus.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.460
No possibility should be overlooked in searching for such a hazard.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:33.960
Perseverance and tedious effort are often necessary in searching for these continuously producing foci.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:40.140
Although such a focus is usually a heavy breeder, it may not be large in size.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.900
When discovered, inspection will reveal all stages of the mosquito.

00:16:53.700 --> 00:17:01.460
The adult should be sprayed immediately to prevent any further infestation. Adults in the vicinity of the focus must also be sprayed.

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:05.900
The mother focus is reported so that permanent correction may be made.

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:15.760
You, as an aegypti inspector, have been assigned important work which must be done thoroughly.

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:26.300
The success of the program depends on your conscientious effort and your ability to integrate Aedes aegypti work with other official sanitation activities.

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:34.100
Just as the aegypti control program depends on you, so must you depend upon gaining the goodwill of the residents within your zone.

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:41.680
Impress upon your people the urgent necessity for mosquito control to protect themselves and their neighbors.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:47.180
Show them in a pleasant and courteous manner the methods of eliminating the aegypti hazard.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:50.500
Encourage each householder to be his own inspector.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:55.720
Thus you will play a vital role in the public health program of your community.

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:06.000
[Music]

